A||N||2||merlin||Carbon Monoxide leak?||||5||
Z||000000||merlin||08-17-2000||09:35 AM||||I own a 1987 GT and I was wondering if carbon monoxide could enter the passenger compartment in a mid engine layout from a possible exhaust leak.  I don’t smell exhaust while I’m driving, but I have some of the tell tail symptoms of carbon monoxide poisoning after driving my Fiero (dizziness, light headed, fatigue).  Maybe I’m just so ecstatic from driving a Fiero.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Seriously though, I could see how carbon monoxide could enter a car where the engine is in front of the driver, but is it possible in a Fiero where the engine is behind the driver?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||08-17-2000||10:13 AM||||You could "borrow" your home CO dectector(you do have one installed in your house right) and try it in the car.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||08-17-2000||11:41 AM||||CO CO2 and other compounds can all build up in a car, any car not just Fiero. That's why nearly all MVAC systems provide some airflow at all times. On many heat/ac systems you can no longer shut them fully off.<P>Fiero is even worse simply because there are so few cubic feet of space inside it. Even if there are no out side contaminates the simple fact you are breathing in a confined space will cause problems in short order if the heat/AC is all off.<P>Fiero does have some passive ventilation but it's not enough to really turn over the cabin air fast enough without help from the heat/ac system. <P>Even if your exhaust is perfect, in traffic you could be picking up from other vehicles.||||reg||1
